From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: where a frame's time goes (stage times, frame
 sizes)

The stream recording only had encode_ms (present to hand-off), so a slow
or uneven stream could not be told apart: a busy GPU delaying capture,
NVENC itself, a full send queue or a large frame paced out slowly all
looked the same.

Every packet now carries when the encoder was handed the frame and when
its bitstream was ready (native NVENC and FFmpeg encoders); the video
broadcast thread notes when it took the packet and when the last packet
left. Heartbeats (metrics, and each stream in sessions) gain p50/p99 of
each stage:

- capture_ms: present to encode start (Desktop Duplication, copy,
  capture queue, colour conversion submit)
- encoder_ms: encode start to bitstream ready (conversion on the GPU and
  the encoder; grows when the games keep the GPU busy)
- queue_ms: bitstream ready to the broadcast thread taking it
- send_ms: packetising, FEC, encryption and paced sending
- sent_ms: present to the last packet sent (plus sent_ms_max)

and frame_kb_avg, frame_kb_max, key_frames, key_frame_kb_max and
repeated_frames (frames sent without new content). Stage values are null
in an interval without samples.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01J21Rm5JX5ZLQfFmc8j2sUj

